SteamARM: a game's FSR 1 upscale done by MetalFX

FSR 1 enlarges a game's 3D picture in two passes, EASU (the upscale) and
RCAS (a sharpening). A fragment shader with the two constants of EASU's
approximations (0x7ef07ebb, 0x5f347d74) is marked when it is compiled
(kk_shader_info.fsr_easu, kept by the pipeline caches); its draw is then
replaced by MetalFX's spatial scaler (MTL4FXSpatialScaler) from the
shader's input texture into the pass's colour attachment, in the same
Metal command buffer: the pass's encoder ends first (its clears and stores
happen), MetalFX follows, and the next encoder waits for all the work
before it (a queue barrier, after_metalfx). RCAS still runs on MetalFX's
result; the HUD drawn afterwards stays at the display's resolution.

The input is found from the descriptor (set 1 binding 30 in Counter-Strike
2's EASU, the sampler at binding 14, the constants at binding 1): image
views register the resource id of their sampled texture. The input is a
texture of the display's size with the picture in its top left; how much of
it comes from EASU's constants (con0: the input's viewport over the output's
size), read from mappable memory registered by GPU address. Constants that
cannot be read: the game's own pass. Measured in Counter-Strike 2 with its
FSR on: picture 1132x637 -> 1920x1080 by MetalFX, about 110 frames a second
on an M4 with its lower settings. Formats or texture
usage MetalFX cannot take, or no Metal 4 MetalFX: the game's own pass.
KK_FSR_METALFX=0: the game's own pass always. tests/elf/vk_fsr.c checks the
replacement with a shader of its own (magenta when drawn, the input
enlarged when MetalFX takes it; its input has the picture in the top left).
--- a/src/kosmickrisp/bridge/meson.build	2026-10-03 07:31:39

Hardened after a review: only a pass drawing to all of level 0 of a plain
2D image from its origin is replaced; MetalFX is asked whether it takes the
pair before the pass is split, and a refusal is remembered; the pass goes
on in a new encoder that waits for MetalFX and ends with the usual producer
barrier, so later command buffers (the WSI's copy) wait for it too; the
dynamic uniform buffer is found through set_dynamic_buffer_start, which
kk_bind_descriptor_sets now keeps; fsr_easu is the shader's own property
and KK_FSR_METALFX is read at draw time (pipeline caches do not know it);
cached scalers drop the game's textures once encoded.

